<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; color: white; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Recycling for Our World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Recycle Plastic Bottles Every Day.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist peers in following multi-step school procedures. Concept: Explain the steps to solve a problem using practical examples.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist peers with recycling procedures by pointing to visuals (e.g., points to the recycling bin and says, "This is for paper."). Can explain steps for solving recycling problems by using practical examples, gestures, and peer collaboration (e.g., "First, sort your trash." and ask, "Can you sort the paper?"). Can simplify steps for recycling using keywords and visuals (e.g., points to each bin and says, "Paper here, plastic here").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays two images on the board: one showing a messy bin with mixed trash and another showing a neatly sorted recycling station with separate bins for plastic, paper, and compost. The teacher asks students to observe and describe what they see. "What do you see in picture 1? What about picture 2? Which one is better for our environment? Why?" Students share their ideas in short sentences, focusing on key vocabulary like 'trash', 'recycling', 'bin', 'plastic', 'paper'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher picks up a plastic bottle and pretends to be confused about where to put it. Then, the teacher models explaining the sorting process using gestures and simple phrases: "Hmm, where does this plastic bottle go? Ah, I know! First, I need to check the bin. Look, this bin says 'Plastic'. So, I can put the plastic bottle HERE." The teacher points clearly to the 'Plastic' bin label and repeats, "Plastic goes HERE. It's easy! We recycle plastic bottles every day." The teacher emphasizes the /r/ sound in "recycle" and models segmenting "environment."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and simple questions to check understanding of the mediation concept (assisting others).
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If your friend doesn't know where to put a paper bag, what can you do? (Point to the paper bin / Say 'Paper here'.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is it okay to mix plastic and paper in one bin? Why not? (No, we need to sort to help the environment.)"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple, illustrated "How to Recycle" guide or a short video showing children setting up and using a recycling station. The guide or video features clear visuals of different bins (plastic, paper, compost) and keywords like 'sort', 'put', 'clean'. The language used will include present simple tense and modal verbs 'can'. Genre: instructional guide/animated instructions. Register: informal and encouraging. Format: visually rich poster or short animated clip.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will understand simple instructions and explanations related to recycling procedures. They will recognize keywords and visual cues to differentiate recycling categories and processes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this guide/video about?" (Expected answer: "How to recycle" or "How to sort trash.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False statements:</b> The teacher gives statements based on the guide. "You can put food scraps in the plastic bin. (False)" "We recycle plastic bottles to help the environment. (True)" Students show thumbs up for true, thumbs down for false.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher shows pictures of items (e.g., a plastic bottle, a newspaper, an apple core) and asks, "Where does this go?" Students point to the corresponding bin on the screen/poster, saying "Plastic bin," "Paper bin," or "Compost bin."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice assisting peers by giving clear, simple instructions and explanations for recycling procedures. They will use present simple for facts, modal 'can' for possibilities, and 'there is/are' to describe the recycling station. Key vocabulary: 'sort', 'plastic', 'paper', 'bottles', 'bin', 'recycle', 'environment'. Pragmatic function: Asking questions "What can we recycle?", giving reasons "We recycle to help the planet." Sociolinguistic: discussing taking care of the environment "We can recycle more."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice - Fill in the blank (Sentence completion):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks based on the recycling guide. Students complete them using target vocabulary. E.g., "We can ______ plastic bottles." (recycle) "First, ______ your trash." (sort) "There ______ a bin for paper." (is). Students write answers individually, then check as a class.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled practice - Picture and Sentence Match:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of different items (e.g., a plastic water bottle, an empty juice carton, a banana peel). For each item, students create a sentence using "You can..." or "This goes to..." and point to a visual representation of the correct bin. E.g., (showing plastic bottle) "You can put this in the plastic bin." or "This goes to the recycling center." The teacher prompts with "Could you clarify that, please?" if needed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction - "Recycling Helper" role-play:</b> Students work in pairs. Student A pretends to be confused about an item (e.g., "Where can I put this glass bottle?"). Student B acts as the "recycling helper" and assists by pointing to a classroom recycling station (or visuals) and giving a simple explanation, e.g., "This is for plastic. We can recycle plastic here." or "First, sort your trash. Then, put paper in this bin." Students switch roles after a few turns. The teacher reminds them to use gestures and clear pronunciation (/r/ in recycle, /s/ in sustainability).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Recycling for Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Group presentation and explanation of their "Recycling Station Poster" to another group. This directly aligns with the 21st Century Skills Project. Students will take turns acting as guides for their poster.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students, in their project groups, finalize their "Recycling Station Posters" (which they started in previous lessons). Each poster should clearly show bins for different materials (plastic, paper, compost) and simple instructions using "You can..." and present simple.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups then pair up with another group (e.g., Group A presents to Group B, then Group B presents to Group A).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One member from the presenting group acts as the main "explainer," pointing to different parts of their poster and explaining the recycling procedures. E.g., "Hello, this is our recycling station. First, you sort your trash. Here, you can put plastic bottles." (pointing) "There is a bin for paper here."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other members of the presenting group assist: one might hold a relevant item (e.g., a plastic bottle) and demonstrate, another might simplify an explanation if the other group looks confused (e.g., "Plastic here, paper here."), and another might ask a clarifying question to the receiving group (e.g., "Can you put food scraps in this bin?") to ensure engagement.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The receiving group asks questions about the poster or recycling process (e.g., "What can we recycle in the blue bin?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on collaboration, clear communication, and successful mediation of the information to peers. The teacher circulates, offering support and prompting.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will demonstrate their ability to assist peers by explaining their "Recycling Station Poster" and clarifying steps for others. The assessment is integrated into the group presentation from Stage 4.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation/Peer Assessment Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Did the student's explanation use clear sounds (e.g., /r/ in "recycle," /s/ in "sustainability")?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Did the student use words like 'plastic', 'bottles', 'recycle', 'sort', 'bin', 'environment'?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and simplicity:</b> Did the student explain the steps clearly and simply, using keywords and visuals? (e.g., "Paper here, plastic here.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration and assistance:</b> Did the student assist peers (pointing, using gestures, asking "Can you sort the paper?")?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar usage:</b> Did the student use present simple ("We recycle") and modal 'can' ("You can recycle") correctly?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick reflection sheet.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I explained well today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I want to improve when helping someone understand recycling is..."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I help my friends understand our recycling poster today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one important thing we learned about recycling to help the planet?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in this mediation lesson? The group presentations allowed for authentic application of the learning outcome. Students were able to use visuals and simple language to explain. What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next time? Perhaps more explicit modeling of asking clarifying questions, e.g., "Could you clarify that?" for the receiving group. Ensuring all students in a group get a chance to be the primary explainer could be a focus.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Recycling for Our World" theme. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, possibly reviewing key vocabulary from this unit as a warm-up or integrating environmental care as a cross-curricular topic.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">___ min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>